// TEST: Duplicate top-level let (rejects)
// ============================================================================
// A second top-level `let` of a name used to pass the checker and then die
// in g++ as a redeclaration -- while the interpreter SHADOWED it, so the two
// lanes disagreed about what the program meant. Refused (BL2009) like a
// duplicate function. (Inside a block, `let` may still shadow; at top level
// an update is an assignment.)
let a = 1.0
let b = a + 1.0
let a = 10.0
let c = a + 1.0
// ERROR: BL2009 @ 9:1
// ERROR-CONTAINS: duplicate declaration of top-level value 'a': this module already declares it at line 7, column 1
